    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint pieces are wired idiomatically \u2014 the exception, handler (matching `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler`/`ProblemDetails` exactly), and YAML step insertion with `govern: [ transaction ]` all fit the existing conventions, and slotting the check before `build` is the right place. One structural issue:\n\n**Duplicated \"owners in this city\" counting logic.** `CheckOwnerCityCapacity.service` computes:\n```java\nlong inCity = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(existing -> city.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getCity()))\n        .count();\n```\nThis is character-for-character the same computation already living in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.service` (lines 24\u201326), which uses that same count to derive the per-city sequence number. The new class's own javadoc admits the coupling (\"City membership is compared case-insensitively, matching how owners-in-city are counted in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`\") \u2014 a comment standing in for shared code. If one definition of \"owners in this city\" ever changes (e.g. trimming/normalizing city like the address normalization elsewhere), the capacity gate at 50 and the sequence numbering will silently disagree.\n\nThis codebase has an established idiom for exactly this: `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique.sameHousehold` is factored out as \"the single source of truth\" and shared with `AssignOwnerHousehold` precisely so the 409 check and the assignment \"can never drift.\" Follow that pattern here \u2014 extract the case-insensitive city count into one shared helper (a static method, or better a `OwnerRepository` query) and have both `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` and `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` call it \u2014 rather than duplicating the stream and relying on a javadoc note to keep them aligned.",
